         <p>A known urn holds 30 red and 60 black balls, so its red probability is \(1/3\) and its black probability is \(2/3\). The bet pays 10000 cents (100 CU) on the stated colour and 0 otherwise, so final wealth is 110000 cents or 100000 cents. Take \(u(w) = -1/w\) (CRRA with \(\gamma = 2\), as in Section 4). With it, the known urn's CE for red is \(103125\) cents, and its CE for black is \(106451.6129\) cents. Under maxmin over the full set \(\{0, \ldots, 90\}\) of red counts, the ambiguous urn's CE is \(100000\) cents for red and \(100000\) cents for black, because the worst prior gives no red and no black ball respectively (Proposition 2.4). The numbers depend on this choice of \(u\), but the preference for the known urn does not. For any strictly increasing \(u\), the worst prior for red, \(P_0\), and the worst prior for black, \(P_{90}\), each give final wealth 100000 cents surely, so the ambiguous CE is 100000 cents for both colours. The known urn's expected utility is \(\tfrac13 u(110000) + \tfrac23 u(100000)\) for red and \(\tfrac23 u(110000) + \tfrac13 u(100000)\) for black, and each exceeds \(u(100000)\), so the known urn's CE exceeds 100000 cents for both colours. So the model prefers the known urn for both colours.</p>
